The '''Joajuaba Tunnel''' ({{langx|bl|Túnel Joajuaba}}, {{langx|kp|Joajuabá Pópe Guatá|}}) is a {{convert|79.84|km|mich|adj=on}} [[W:Standard gauge|standard-gauge]] railway tunnel in [[Balisca]], with a {{convert|56.42|km|mich|adj=on}} segment running beneath the seabed of the [[Salsipuedes Sea]], which separates the island of [[Pindorama]], on the strait's western shore, from the island [[Sicania]] on it's eastern shore. The tunnel's track level lies approximately {{convert|55|m|-1}} below the seabed and {{convert|100|m}} below sea level.

The Joajuaba Tunnel forms part of the standard-gauge [[Pindorama high speed rail line|HSR Pindorama]] as well as the freight [[Pirakue Line]] operated by ''[[Transatlántida|Transpindorama]]'' (BR North), and forms a critical portion of the [[Trans-Archipelago Link]].

By total length, the Joajuaba Tunnel is the world’s [[W:List of longest tunnels|longest undersea tunnel]], surpassing both the [[W:Seikan Tunnel|Seikan Tunnel]] and the [[W:Channel Tunnel|Channel Tunnel]], with it's undersea section surpassing the total length of both. It is the second [[W:List of longest railway tunnels|longest main-line railway tunnel]], following Switzerland’s [[W:Gotthard Base Tunnel|Gotthard Base Tunnel]].

